-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the series representing a rigidification's isogeny
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$, a natural number $N$, a commutative ring $\mathcal O$ with an element $\pi$, and a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; write $k_0 = O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ for the quotient by the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$. Fix rationals $a,b$, a $\mathbb Z$-submodule $\Lambda$ of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$, a map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathrm{Zp2}\,r \times \mathrm{Zp2}\,r$ into pairs of Witt vectors over the field of $r^2$ elements, a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ of level $N$ over $k_0$ (an abelian scheme of relative dimension $2$ with commutative group law, $\Lambda$-action and level structure), and formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of rank $2$ for its structure morphism. Fix further a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, a fake elliptic curve $E$ of level $N$ over $B$, a rigidification $\rho$ of $E$ relative to $\pi$, $\psi$ and $A_0$ (consisting of reductions $E_b$, $A_b$ over $\bar B = B/(\pi)$ together with the pull-back morphisms $g_b$, $g_A$, an integer $d$ and an $r^d$-isogeny pair $\varphi, \varphi'$ between $E_b$ and $A_b$ preserving level), a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ over $B$ for $r$, and formal coordinates $\theta$ of rank $2$ for $E$ with $E$.`IsFormalModuleVia` $\mathrm{coord}$ $X$ $\theta$, i.e. $\theta$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $E$ with formal group $X.F$ and the action of each $m \in \Lambda$ on nilpotent points is computed by the series $\mathrm{addVia}$ of $X.\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}\,m)_1$ and $X.\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}\,m)_2 \circ X.\varpi$. Let $\sigma_1, \sigma_2$ be pairs of formal power series in two variables over $\bar B$, all of whose constant coefficients vanish, and suppose each $\sigma_j$ satisfies the following representation clause: for every commutative ring $B''$ carrying compatible $B$-, $\bar B$- and $k_0$-algebra structures, in the sense that the map $B \to B''$ is the map $B \to \bar B$ followed by $\bar B \to B''$ and the map $k_0 \to B''$ is the residue leg $k_0 \to \bar B$ induced by $\psi$ followed by $\bar B \to B''$, for every ideal $J$ of $B''$ and $m$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B''$ with all $s_i \in J$, and every morphism $P_A : \operatorname{Spec} B'' \to \rho.A_b$ over $\bar B$ whose composite with $\rho.g_A$ is the point $\theta_0(B'')(s)$, the composite of $P_A$ with $\rho.\varphi'$ followed by $\rho.g_b$ equals the point $\theta(B'')$ evaluated at the coordinatewise degree-$m$ truncated evaluations of $\sigma_j$ at $s$. Then $\sigma_1 = \sigma_2$.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the coordinate description of the isogeny $\varphi'$ of a rigidification: the pair of power series over $\bar B$ with vanishing constant terms that computes $\varphi'$ in the fixed formal coordinates $\theta_0$ on $A_b$ and $\theta$ on $E_b$ is determined by that property. It is used in the study of rigidification transports and of admissible rigidifications over local and Artinian bases.
